"Private Client Use Cases | SCAPI | B2C Commerce Storefront Reference Architecture | Salesforce Developers (https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/commerce/sfra/guide/slas-private-client.html)\nciteturn8view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/commerce/sfra/guide/slas-private-client.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 94\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMCP server – Linear Docs (https://linear.app/docs/mcp)\nciteturn8view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6view7\",\"lineno\":60}); Total lines: 402\nL0: cite0†Skip to content → L1: \nL2: cite1†Docs L3: \nL4: cite2†Sign up cite2†Open app L5: \nL6:   * Getting started\nL7:   * Account\nL8:   * AI\nL9: \nL10:     * cite3†Linear Agent L11:     * cite4†Loops L12:     * cite5†AI Agents L13:     * cite6†Assign and delegate issues L14:     * cite7†Triage Intelligence L15:     * cite8†Code Intelligence L16:     * cite9†Coding sessions L17:     * cite10†MCP server L18: \nL19:   * Your sidebar\nL20:   * Teams\nL21:   * Issues\nL22:   * Issue properties\nL23:   * Projects\nL24:   * Initiatives\nL25:   * Cycles\nL26:   * Views\nL27:   * Find and filter\nL28:   * Linear Asks\nL29:   * Integrations\nL30:     * cite11†Integration Directory L31:     * cite10†MCP server L32:     * cite5†AI Agents L33:     * cite12†Airbyte L34:     * cite13†Linear Asks L35:     * cite14†Discord L36:     * cite15†Figma L37:     * cite16†Front L38:     * cite17†GitHub L39:     * cite18†GitLab L40:     * cite19†Google Sheets L41:     * cite20†Gong L42:     * cite21†Intercom L43:     * cite22†Jira L44:     * cite23†Microsoft Teams L45:     * cite24†Notion L46:     * cite25†Salesforce L47:     * cite26†Sentry L48:     * cite27†Slack L49:     * cite28†Zapier L50:     * cite29†Zendesk L51: \nL52:   * Analytics\nL53:   * Administration\nL54:   * Importers\nL55: cite1†Docs cite30†Developers cite31†Learn cite32†Contact support L56: \nL57:   1. AI\nL58:   2. cite33†MCP server L59: \nL60: Copy page\nL61: \nL62: cite2†Sign up cite2†Open app L63: # MCP server\nL64: \nL65: cite34†Image: Abstract image of a drive with Linear's logo and the words \"Remote MCP server\"†webassets.linear.app L66: \nL67: The Model Context Protocol (MCP) server provides a standardized interface that allows any compatible AI model or agent to access your Linear data in a simple and secure way.\nL68: \nL69: Connect to our MCP server natively in Claude, Cursor, and other clients or use the cite35†`mcp-remote`†github.com module for backwards compatibility with clients that do not support remote MCP.\nL70: Linear’s MCP server follows the authenticated remote cite36†MCP spec†modelcontextprotocol.io , so the server is centrally hosted and managed. The Linear MCP server has tools available for finding, creating, and updating objects in Linear like issues, projects, and comments — with more functionality on the way, and cite32†feedback on its functionality is welcomed.\nL71: \nL72: For help getting started after setup, see some of our cite37†example prompts .\nL73: ## Setupcite38†⁠ L74: ### Generalcite39†⁠ L75: \nL76: Our MCP server uses Streamable HTTP as the primary transport, accessible at the following address. The interactive setup flow uses OAuth 2.1 with dynamic client registration. You can also authenticate directly with a bearer token or Linear API key — see the FAQ for details.\nL77: \nL78: Read-write access is provided through `https://mcp.linear.app/mcp` by default.\nL79: \nL80: For read-only access, you have two options:\nL81:   * Connect to `https://mcp.linear.app/mcp/readonly`, which only ever exposes read tools.\nL82:   * Use the standard `/mcp` endpoint, but only request the `read` OAuth scope. Clients that request `read` are granted read-only access, and the underlying token can’t reach write APIs.\nL83: \nL84: For instructions for specific clients, read on. Dedicated setup pages are also available for cite40†Claude , cite41†Cursor , cite42†VS Code , cite43†Windsurf , and cite44†Zed .\nL85: ### Claudecite45†⁠ L86: \nL87: Team, Enterprise (Claude.ai)\nL88: \nL89: Navigate to the cite46†connectors†claude.ai page and connect Linear.\nL90: \nL91: Free, Pro (Claude desktop)\nL92: \nL93: From Claude settings > Connectors you can add the Linear connector.\nL94: #### Enterprise-managed authorizationcite47†⁠ L95: \nL96: If you use Okta, first configure SAML for Linear using cite48†Okta’s OIN . Then enable MCP enterprise managed authentication from the bottom of your Okta identity provider settings in Linear.\nL97: \nL98: cite49†Image: MCP Enterprise managed authentication settings located at the bottom of Okta Configuration Settings†webassets.linear.app L99: Once this is toggled on, copy the cite50†Issuer URI†developer.okta.com for the authorization server from Okta’s side and paste this into your Linear settings:\nL100: \nL101:     `e.g. https://your-org.okta.com`\nL102: \nL103: cite51†Image: Okta API Settings for Authorization Server Issuer URL†webassets.linear.app L104: \nL105: cite51†Image: Okta API Settings for Authorization Server Issuer URL†webassets.linear.app L106: \nL107: This allows supported external MCP clients such as Claude to authenticate users automatically using your Okta-managed access policies.\nL108: ### Claude Codecite52†⁠ L109: \nL110:     `claude mcp add --transport http linear-server https://mcp.linear.app/mcp`\nL111: \nL112: Then run `/mcp` once you’ve opened a Claude Code session to go through the authentication flow.\nL113: ### Codexcite53†⁠ L114: \nL115: The setup steps for the MCP server are the same regardless of whether you use the IDE Extension or the CLI since the configuration is shared.\nL116: \nL117: Configuration via CLI:\nL118: \nL119: Run the following command in Terminal:\nL120: \nL121:     `codex mcp add linear --url https://mcp.linear.app/mcp`\nL122: \nL123: This will automatically prompt you to log in with your Linear account and connect it to your Codex.\nL124: Note: If this is the first time you are using an MCP in Codex you will need to enable the `rmcp` feature for this to work. Add the following into your `~/.codex/config.toml`:\nL125: \nL126:     `[features]\nL127:     experimental_use_rmcp_client = true`\nL128: \nL129: Configuration through environment variables:\nL130: \nL131:   1. Open the `~/.codex/config.toml` file in your preferred editor\nL132:   2. Add the following:\nL133: \nL134:     `[features]\nL135:     experimental_use_rmcp_client = true\nL136: \nL137:     [mcp_servers.linear]\nL138:     url = \"https://mcp.linear.app/mcp\"`\nL139: Run `codex mcp login linear` to move through the authentication flow.\nL140: ### Cursorcite54†⁠ L141: \nL142: To add the MCP to Cursor, you can install by clicking here, or searching for Linear from Cursor’s cite55†MCP tools page†cursor.com .\nL143: \nL144: cite56†Image: C†webassets.linear.app L145: \nL146: ### Julescite57†⁠ L147: \nL148:   * In Linear, go to Settings > Account > cite58†Security & Access L149:   * Generate a new API key and copy it somewhere secure\nL150:   * Now in Jules, go to cite59†MCP Settings†jules.google.com L151:   * Select Connect on the Linear server and paste your API key\nL152: ### Visual Studio Codecite60†⁠ L153: \nL154:     `{\nL155:       \"mcpServers\": {\nL156:         \"linear\": {\nL157:           \"command\": \"npx\",\nL158:           \"args\": [\"-y\", \"mcp-remote\", \"https://mcp.linear.app/mcp\"]\nL159:         }\nL160:       }\nL161:     }`\nL162: \nL163:   1. `CTRL/CMD `` P` and search for MCP: Add Server.\nL164:   2. Select Command (stdio)\nL165:   3. Enter the following configuration, and hit enter.\nL166: \nL167: `npx mcp-remote https://mcp.linear.app/mcp`\nL168:   1. Enter the name Linear and hit enter.\nL169:   2. Activate the server using MCP: List Servers and selecting Linear, and selecting Start Server.\nL170: ### v0 by Vercelcite61†⁠ L171: \nL172: To add the MCP to v0, you can install from the cite62†connections†v0.app page.\nL173: \nL174: ### Windsurfcite63†⁠ L175: \nL176:   1. `CTRL/CMD `` ,` to open Windsurf settings.\nL177:   2. Under Scroll to Cascade -> MCP servers\nL178:   3. Select Add Server -> Add custom server\nL179:   4. Add the following:\nL180: \nL181:     `{\nL182:       \"mcpServers\": {\nL183:         \"linear\": {\nL184:           \"command\": \"npx\",\nL185:           \"args\": [\"-y\", \"mcp-remote\", \"https://mcp.linear.app/mcp\"]\nL186:         }\nL187:       }\nL188:     }`\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOverview - Clay docs (https://developers.clay.com/)\nciteturn8view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view0\",\"lineno\":35}); Total lines: 84\nL0: > ## Documentation Index\nL1: >\nL2: > Fetch the complete documentation index at: cite0†/llms.txt L3: >\nL4: > Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\nL5: \nL6: cite1†Skip to main content L7: \nL8: cite2†Clay docs home page L9: \nL10: Search...\nL11: \nL12: Navigation\nL13: \nL14: Start here\nL15: \nL16: Overview\nL17: # Overview\nL18: \nL19: Use Clay from agents, terminals, backend services, internal tools, and custom apps.\nL20: \nL21: Clay used to live primarily in the Clay UI. Now Clay also works where your agents, developers, and business systems already are. Use the developer platform to search Clay’s GTM data, run reusable Clay logic, read table data, and work with Audiences programmatically.\nL22: \nL23: ##\nL24: \nL25: cite3†​ L26: \nL27: Start here\nL28: ## Install the Clay plugin\nL29: \nL30: Best for Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, and agent-first work. The plugin ships skills and the Clay CLI.\nL31: \nL32: ## Call the API directly\nL33: \nL34: Best for backend jobs, internal tools, custom apps, queues, and product workflows.\nL35: ##\nL36: \nL37: cite4†​ L38: \nL39: Core primitives\nL40: \nL41: Primitive  | Use it for\nL42: --- | ---\nL43: cite5†Searches | Find companies and people using Clay’s GTM database\nL44: cite6†Routines | Run Clay-managed functions, custom functions, and Workflows\nL45: cite7†Tables (Enterprise) | Query existing Clay table data from known tables\nL46: cite8†Audiences | Work with records, saved segments, activities, and signal events\nL47: cite9†Signals | Create triggers that detect relevant changes across records\nL48: Start with the primitive that matches where your data lives and what you want to do.\nL49: ##\nL50: \nL51: cite10†​ L52: \nL53: Choose the right primitive\nL54: \nL55:   * Need a target list? Use cite5†Searches .\nL56:   * Need enrichment, scoring, routing, or reusable logic? Use cite6†Routines .\nL57:   * Need existing table data? Use cite7†Tables . Tables are Enterprise only.\nL58:   * Need Audiences records, saved segments, activities, or signal events? Use cite8†Audiences .\nL59:   * Need triggers that watch records and produce signal events? Use cite9†Signals .\nL60: \nL61: ##\nL62: \nL63: cite11†​ L64: \nL65: Start with a recipe\nL66: ## Run a search, then enrich with a function\nL67: \nL68: Pull a target list from Clay data, then run Clay-managed functions on the results.\nL69: \nL70: ## Give an agent access to a Clay function\nL71: \nL72: Let an agent call Clay logic as part of its normal work.\nL73: \nL74: ## Build a Workflow (Alpha)\nL75: \nL76: Create, edit, validate, run, and inspect a Workflow from the plugin or CLI.\nL77: ## Analyze recent closed-won deals\nL78: \nL79: Use an agent to find patterns in recent successful deals.\nL80: \nL81: cite12†Quickstart Build with Clay from your coding agent, or call the Clay API from your own applications.Next L82: \nL83: cite13†Powered byThis documentation is built and hosted on Mintlify, a developer documentation platform†www.mintlify.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAPI Accounts | BigCommerce Docs (https://developer.bigcommerce.com/docs/start/authentication)\nciteturn8view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"pattern\":\"Merchants generate\"}); Redirected to URL: https://docs.bigcommerce.com/developer/docs/overview/api-fundamentals/api-accounts; Total lines: 1209\nL668: Every parent set of API credentials that you request for your store is its own API account. At its simplest, an API account consists of the following:\nL669: \nL670:   * The `client_id` uniquely identifies the app or user, or the client, making a request.\nL671:   * The `client_secret` is a cryptographically secure value known only to the client and BigCommerce.\nL672: Every active API account has at least one `access_token`. cite572†Store-level API accounts include a static access token that does not change. cite578†App-level API accounts expect each application to generate a unique access token for every store that installs the app. cite583†Account-level API accounts include a single access token that belongs to the parent account of the store that the user is signed in to at the time of creation.\nL673: Guard these values closely. The client ID and client secret will never change; access tokens do not expire based on time and cannot be manually invalidated. It’s best practice to limit each account’s cite590†OAuth scope to only the privileges needed to complete that app or user’s designated tasks. Create separate API accounts for each app, store API user or function, or account-related implementation.\nL674: ### Choosing the right kind of API account\nL675: \nL676: Where both types of API account are supported, review the preceding sections to make an informed choice about which best fits your use case.\nL677: API or Use Case  | Store-level API account  | App-level API account  | Account-level API account\nL678: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL679: cite596†GraphQL Account API , including cite613†Users |  |  | ×\nL680: cite596†GraphQL Admin API | ×  | ×  |\nL681: cite603†GraphQL Storefront API | ×  |  |\nL682: cite596†B2B REST Management API | ×  |  |\nL683: cite596†REST Store Management APIs | ×  | ×  |\nL684: cite608†REST Storefront API | ×  |  |\nL685: cite605†Customer Login API |  | ×  |\nL686: cite599†Current Customer API |  | ×  |\nL687: cite603†Payments API | ×  | ×  |\nL688: cite610†Apps that host REST Provider APIs (provider apps) |  | ×  |\nL689: cite614†Apps that create App Extensions |  | ×  |\nL690: Apps hosted in the store control panel (single-click apps)  |  | ×  |\nL691: Manual connection between a third-party app and a store  | ×  |  | ×\nL692: Single-store frontend scripts  |  | ×  |\nL693: ### Store-level API accounts\nL694: \nL695: Merchants generate single-store API credentials when they create API accounts in their store control panel, in the cite615†Settings > Store-level API accounts†login.bigcommerce.com menu. Use these credentials to read and change one store’s data with BigCommerce’s APIs. You can’t change store-level access tokens or OAuth scopes.\nL696: \nL697: In addition to the cite570†API account components in the preceding section, store-level API accounts contain the following attributes out of the box:\nL698:   * An `access_token`, which accompanies most REST API requests.\nL699:   * The client name is a label for your convenience, and it doesn’t accompany requests.\nL700:   * The API path is the URL to which you make requests. The API path won’t change, but it will have `/v3/` or `/v2/` appended to it, depending on the current version for the endpoint you’re querying.\nL701: Most APIs that work with store-level API accounts use the `access_token` to authenticate requests to BigCommerce. However, a few use the access token to generate a temporary credential. To learn more about special cases that involve store-level credentials, consult the documentation for the API you want to use.\nL702: #### Be careful with client secrets\nL703: \nL704: Do not send your `client_secret` or `access_token` in plain text or an unencrypted payload. Be particularly careful with the `client_secret`. An attacker can use your `client_secret` to both sign and decrypt JWTs sent between you and BigCommerce.\nL705: \nL706: #### Creating store-level API credentials\nL707: \nL708: To create a store-level API account, consult our Knowledge Base article on cite616†Creating a Store API Account†support.bigcommerce.com .\nL709: #### Revoking store-level API credentials\nL710: \nL711: To revoke store-level API credentials, you must delete the corresponding store API account. If the `client_id` and `client_secret` are compromised, or the account has become unnecessary, secure your account by deleting the API account. You cannot recover a deleted API account, so take care.\nL712: #### Delete carefully\nL713: \nL714: Deleting an account cannot be undone, so be sure before clicking the trash can icon. You can also use the checkboxes on the left side to delete multiple accounts at once – but be especially careful when using this option.\nL715: \nL716: To delete a store-level API account, consult our Knowledge Base article on cite617†Deleting a Store API Account†support.bigcommerce.com .\nL717: #### Don’t forget your webhooks and metafields\nL718: \nL719: Some resources are only accessible to the API account that created them. These include webhooks, metafields, and scripts. If you need to revoke a store-level API account, plan accordingly.\nL720: \nL721: Webhooks do not persist upon account deletion. Verify webhooks associated with an account before deleting it.\nL722: ### App-level API accounts\nL723: \nL724: You can cite579†create app-level API accounts in the cite618†Developer Portal†build.bigcommerce.com . After a store owner or authorized user installs and authorizes an app, most apps use access tokens generated from the API account’s client ID, client secret, and a temporary grant code to read and change store data. cite580†Generate access tokens with the BigCommerce-initiated grant code authorization flow.\nL725: Some APIs use app-level API accounts to implement alternative authentication patterns. For a summary of all our authentication methods, see the cite595†authentication schemes described earlier in this article.\nL726: \nL727: For more on working with apps, see our cite347†Guide to Building Apps . The sections on cite356†Implementing OAuth and cite357†Callback Handlers are particularly relevant to generating access tokens.\nL728: #### Creating app-level API credentials\nL729: \nL730: To create an app and its associated API account, consult our article on cite619†Managing Apps in the Developer Portal .\nL731: \nL732: #### App-level access tokens\nL733: \nL734: App-level API accounts do not come pre-configured with an access token. Each time a store installs your app, BigCommerce initiates a grant code authorization flow to help your app generate a dedicated access token for that store. For further details, see cite356†Authenticating an app .\nL735: #### Revoking app-level API credentials\nL736: \nL737: There is no way to manually revoke or force-regenerate app-level API account access tokens. However, either of the following actions triggers a token refresh:\nL738: \nL739:   * When the store owner’s email address changes\nL740:   * When you modify the app API account’s OAuth scopes\nL741: \nL742: After one of these changes, the store owner will be prompted to review the change and reauthorize the app the next time they click the app icon in the store control panel.\nL743: #### Delete apps carefully\nL744: \nL745: When you delete an app in the cite618†Dev Portal†build.bigcommerce.com , there is no way to recover the client ID or client secret. If you choose to do this, don’t forget to mitigate the potential loss of cite577†webhook and metafield -related data and functionality.\nL746: \nL747: To delete an app-level API account, consult our article on cite620†Managing Apps in the Developer Portal .\nL748: ### Account-level API accounts\nL749: Account-level API accounts are designed to work with the GraphQL Account API, which manages resources beyond the scope of a single store. For example, cite613†managing control panel users in the BigCommerce ecosystem. Current and future features available using the GraphQL Account API toolset allow developers to build custom integrations and solutions that require high-level access to store and platform resources.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAPI Accounts | BigCommerce Docs (https://developer.bigcommerce.com/docs/start/authentication)\nciteturn8view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"pattern\":\"BigCommerce offers a suite\"}); Redirected to URL: https://docs.bigcommerce.com/developer/docs/overview/api-fundamentals/api-accounts; Total lines: 1209\nL561:   * cite522†Third party API proxy L562:   * cite13†Closed Beta Programs L563:   * cite523†Overview L564:   * cite524†API Reference L580:   * cite540†API Reference L581:   * cite541†List Accounts L582:   * cite542†Create Account L583:   * cite543†Get Account L584:   * cite544†Update Account L585:   * cite545†Create Account with Set Password L586:   * cite546†Create Store L587:   * cite547†Get Store L588:   * cite548†Create Store Login URL L589:   * cite549†List Stores L590:   * cite550†Create Store Plan Change L591:   * cite551†Create Store Cancellation L592:   * cite552†Create Store Suspension L593:   * cite553†Create Store Reactivation L594:   * cite554†Get OAuth Token L595:   * cite555†Overview L596:   * cite556†Getting Started L597:   * cite557†Catalog Data L598:   * cite558†ML Models L599:   * cite559†Request Recommendations L600:   * cite560†Search L601:   * cite561†Shopper Events L602:   * cite562†Stencil Headless Catalyst L603:   * cite563†API Reference L604:   * cite564†Update Google Vertex Ai Settings L605:   * cite565†Delete Google Vertex Ai Settings L606:   * cite566†Overview L607:   * cite567†HTTP Webhooks L608:   * cite568†Postman Collection L609:   * cite14†AI Agent Setup L610: cite569†Dev Portal†build.bigcommerce.com L611: \nL612: Light\nL613: \nL614: On this page\nL615:   * cite570†API accounts L616:   * cite571†Choosing the right kind of API account L617:   * cite572†Store-level API accounts L618:   * cite573†Be careful with client secrets L619:   * cite574†Creating store-level API credentials L620:   * cite575†Revoking store-level API credentials L621:   * cite576†Delete carefully L622:   * cite577†Don’t forget your webhooks and metafields L623:   * cite578†App-level API accounts L624:   * cite579†Creating app-level API credentials L625:   * cite580†App-level access tokens L626:   * cite581†Revoking app-level API credentials L627:   * cite582†Delete apps carefully L628:   * cite583†Account-level API accounts L629:   * cite584†Creating account-level credentials L630:   * cite585†Revoking account-level API credentials L631:   * cite586†Migrating from legacy to OAuth L632:   * cite587†Legacy API Accounts L633:   * cite588†Benefits of migrating to OAuth L634:   * cite589†How to migrate L635:   * cite590†OAuth scopes L636:   * cite591†Token creation scopes L637:   * cite592†Store resource scopes L638:   * cite593†Account resource scopes L639:   * cite594†Authentication L640:   * cite595†Stable tokens L641:   * cite596†Access tokens L642:   * cite597†The X-Auth-Client header is deprecated L643:   * cite598†X-Auth-Token header example requests L644:   * cite599†Client ID L645:   * cite600†Current Customer API example request L646:   * cite601†Dynamic tokens L647:   * cite602†Make sure your tokens are in scope L648:   * cite603†BigCommerce-generated JWTs L649:   * cite604†Authorization header example requests L650:   * cite605†User-generated JWTs L651:   * cite606†Customer Login API example request L652:   * cite607†BigCommerce-encrypted payload JWTs L653:   * cite608†Same-origin CORS authentication L654:   * cite609†REST Storefront API L655:   * cite610†Developer-configured authentication L656:   * cite611†REST Provider APIs L657:   * cite612†Resources L658: Scroll to top\nL659: # API Accounts\nL660: \nL661: BigCommerce offers a suite of APIs that let you manage store data, sign customers in, make client-side queries for product information, and write apps that integrate third-party services into store operations.\nL662: BigCommerce API accounts play a role in every authenticated request to our servers. Our API accounts come in a few different flavors to meet the needs of different use cases. For example, app API accounts work well in multi-store contexts, whereas store API accounts are a good choice for frontend applications. Some endpoints only work with one kind of API account.\nL663: \nL664: For an overview of the authentication schemes that our APIs use, see the cite594†Authentication section below.\nL665: ## API accounts\nL666: \nL667: BigCommerce offers three types of OAuth-based API accounts to developers who wish to use BigCommerce’s REST APIs: store-level credentials, app-level credentials, and account-level credentials. This section describes the difference between the three, how to obtain and revoke account credentials, and the use cases for each. It also contains a reference for available OAuth scopes and provides a compelling list of reasons to migrate from legacy API tokens to OAuth credentials.\nL668: Every parent set of API credentials that you request for your store is its own API account. At its simplest, an API account consists of the following:\nL669: \nL670:   * The `client_id` uniquely identifies the app or user, or the client, making a request.\nL671:   * The `client_secret` is a cryptographically secure value known only to the client and BigCommerce.\nL672: Every active API account has at least one `access_token`. cite572†Store-level API accounts include a static access token that does not change. cite578†App-level API accounts expect each application to generate a unique access token for every store that installs the app. cite583†Account-level API accounts include a single access token that belongs to the parent account of the store that the user is signed in to at the time of creation.\nL673: Guard these values closely. The client ID and client secret will never change; access tokens do not expire based on time and cannot be manually invalidated. It’s best practice to limit each account’s cite590†OAuth scope to only the privileges needed to complete that app or user’s designated tasks. Create separate API accounts for each app, store API user or function, or account-related implementation.\nL674: ### Choosing the right kind of API account\nL675: \nL676: Where both types of API account are supported, review the preceding sections to make an informed choice about which best fits your use case.\nL677: API or Use Case  | Store-level API account  | App-level API account  | Account-level API account\nL678: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL679: cite596†GraphQL Account API , including cite613†Users |  |  | ×\nL680: cite596†GraphQL Admin API | ×  | ×  |\nL681: cite603†GraphQL Storefront API | ×  |  |\nL682: cite596†B2B REST Management API | ×  |  |\nL683: cite596†REST Store Management APIs | ×  | ×  |\nL684: cite608†REST Storefront API | ×  |  |\nL685: cite605†Customer Login API |  | ×  |\nL686: cite599†Current Customer API |  | ×  |\nL687: cite603†Payments API | ×  | ×  |\nL688: cite610†Apps that host REST Provider APIs (provider apps) |  | ×  |\nL689: cite614†Apps that create App Extensions |  | ×  |\nL690: Apps hosted in the store control panel (single-click apps)  |  | ×  |\nL691: Manual connection between a third-party app and a store  | ×  |  | ×\nL692: Single-store frontend scripts  |  | ×  |\nL693: ### Store-level API accounts\nL694: \nL695: Merchants generate single-store API credentials when they create API accounts in their store control panel, in the cite615†Settings > Store-level API accounts†login.bigcommerce.com menu. Use these credentials to read and change one store’s data with BigCommerce’s APIs. You can’t change store-level access tokens or OAuth scopes.\nL696: \nL697: In addition to the cite570†API account components in the preceding section, store-level API accounts contain the following attributes out of the box:\nL698:   * An `access_token`, which accompanies most REST API requests.\nL699:   * The client name is a label for your convenience, and it doesn’t accompany requests.\nL700:   * The API path is the URL to which you make requests. The API path won’t change, but it will have `/v3/` or `/v2/` appended to it, depending on the current version for the endpoint you’re querying.\nL701: Most APIs that work with store-level API accounts use the `access_token` to authenticate requests to BigCommerce. However, a few use the access token to generate a temporary credential. To learn more about special cases that involve store-level credentials, consult the documentation for the API you want to use.\nL702: #### Be careful with client secrets\nL703: \nL704: Do not send your `client_secret` or `access_token` in plain text or an unencrypted payload. Be particularly careful with the `client_secret`. An attacker can use your `client_secret` to both sign and decrypt JWTs sent between you and BigCommerce.\nL705: \nL706: #### Creating store-level API credentials\nL707: \nL708: To create a store-level API account, consult our Knowledge Base article on cite616†Creating a Store API Account†support.bigcommerce.com .\nL709: #### Revoking store-level API credentials\nL710: \nL711: To revoke store-level API credentials, you must delete the corresponding store API account. If the `client_id` and `client_secret` are compromised, or the account has become unnecessary, secure your account by deleting the API account. You cannot recover a deleted API account, so take care.\nL712: #### Delete carefully\nL713: \nL714: Deleting an account cannot be undone, so be sure before clicking the trash can icon. You can also use the checkboxes on the left side to delete multiple accounts at once – but be especially careful when using this option.\nL715: \nL716: To delete a store-level API account, consult our Knowledge Base article on cite617†Deleting a Store API Account†support.bigcommerce.com .\nL717: #### Don’t forget your webhooks and metafields\nL718: \nL719: Some resources are only accessible to the API account that created them. These include webhooks, metafields, and scripts. If you need to revoke a store-level API account, plan accordingly.\nL720: \nL721: Webhooks do not persist upon account deletion. Verify webhooks associated with an account before deleting it.\n"
